# What happens to a file after you upload it to deploy/.
# Rendered by scripts/render-diagrams.sh — edit the .d2, not the .svg.

direction: down

op: "You, in a bucket browser\nor any S3 client" { shape: person }

s1: "1. Upload\ns3://…/alice/deploy/parcels.gpkg" {
  style.fill: "#FFF9E6"
  style.stroke: "#E8A33D"
}

s2: "2. Next sync tick\ncopied into ~/Desktop/parcels.gpkg\nas uid 1000, not root" {
  style.fill: "#E8F4FB"
  style.stroke: "#1E88C7"
}

s3: "3. Immediately after\ndeleted from deploy/" {
  style.fill: "#E8F4FB"
  style.stroke: "#1E88C7"
}

s4: "4. Next save\nmirrored back as\nhome/Desktop/parcels.gpkg" {
  style.fill: "#1E88C7"
  style.font-color: "#FFFFFF"
  style.stroke: "#1E88C7"
}

done: "The user has the file,\nand deploy/ is empty again" {
  style.fill: "#F5F5F2"
  style.bold: true
}

op -> s1
s1 -> s2: "while the\ncontainer runs" {
  style.stroke: "#E8A33D"
}
s2 -> s3: "delivered once,\nnever again"
s3 -> s4
s4 -> done

wrong: |md
  **If the container is offline**, or the
  copy fails, nothing is deleted: the file
  waits in `deploy/` and is retried on the
  next cycle.

  **The mistake to avoid**

  Uploading into `home/` instead does
  NOT hand the file over. `home/` is a
  mirror of the container, one way only:
  a file that is in the bucket and not on
  the desktop looks exactly like one the
  user deleted, so the next save removes
  it again — into `.persist-trash/`.

  Step 4 is why `deploy/` can be a
  letterbox rather than storage: once
  delivered, the file is simply one of
  the user's own files, and the ordinary
  mirror keeps it.
| {
  near: bottom-center
  style.fill: "#FFF9E6"
}
